WEBVTT

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:03.140
Tokens per second describes approximate generation speed,

00:00:03.140 --> 00:00:07.294
while prompt-processing speed describes how quickly input context is evaluated.

00:00:07.294 --> 00:00:10.330
A system can generate quickly yet pause for a long prompt,

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.140
or process prompts quickly while generating slowly.

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:17.220
Batch settings can improve throughput but increase memory use and may affect latency.

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:21.580
Concurrency changes the problem again: several users or jobs compete for compute, cache,

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:22.220
and memory.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:26.095
Benchmark expected simultaneous demand, not only one ideal request.

00:00:26.095 --> 00:00:30.600
GPU offload moves supported model layers or operations from CPU execution to an

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:31.480
accelerator.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.085
More offload often improves speed until memory pressure, transfer overhead,

00:00:35.085 --> 00:00:36.985
or backend limits dominate.

00:00:36.985 --> 00:00:38.960
The optimum is configuration-specific.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.000
Context length should match the application.

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:44.760
Loading maximum context for short tasks wastes cache and prompt-processing time.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.225
Retrieval, summarization, code, and chat workloads have different useful context patterns.

00:00:49.225 --> 00:00:52.845
Memory pressure can cause allocation failures, swapping, severe slowdown,

00:00:52.845 --> 00:00:54.001
or service instability.

00:00:54.001 --> 00:00:57.720
Thermal throttling can reduce sustained speed after an impressive short benchmark.

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.360
Monitor temperatures, clocks, memory, power, latency, errors, and queue depth over time.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:06.615
Benchmark practical workloads with representative prompts, output lengths, context,

00:01:06.615 --> 00:01:09.843
models, quantizations, concurrency, and acceptance criteria.

00:01:09.843 --> 00:01:11.280
Record quality as well as speed.

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:13.520
The best setup completes real work reliably;

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.400
it does not merely win a synthetic tokens-per-second comparison.
